{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "317fc248-dee8-522d-9f3a-ce7380dbab1b" :question/math-academy-id "q-332439" :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}^{′}(t)=5{x}_{1}(t)-{x}_{2}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}^{′}(t)=-5{x}_{1}(t)+{x}_{2}(t),\\end{aligned}\\,x(0)=[\\begin{aligned}5 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nConsider the initial value problem above. By applying the Laplace transform to both sides of each equation in our system, we get a transformed system with the solution \n\n$$\n{X}_{1}(s)=L\\{{x}_{1}(t)\\}=\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{4}{s-6},\\,{X}_{2}(s)=L\\{{x}_{2}(t)\\}=\\frac{5}{s}-\\frac{4}{s-6}.\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the original initial value problem.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$x(t)=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-1}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,+\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-2}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" rowspan=\"2\">\n$\\,\\,{e}^{6t}$\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-3}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td height=\"30px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">\n{{field-4}}\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>" :question/worked-solution "To find ${x}_{1}(t)$ and ${x}_{2}(t)$ satisfying the original initial value problem, we apply the inverse Laplace transform to ${X}_{1}(s)$ and ${X}_{2}(s):$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{1}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}+\\frac{4}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}+4{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =1+4{e}^{6\\cdot t} \\\\  & =1+4{e}^{6t} \\\\ {x}_{2}(t) & ={L}^{-1}\\{{X}_{2}(s)\\} \\\\  & ={L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{5}{s}-\\frac{4}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =5{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s}\\}-4{L}^{-1}\\{\\frac{1}{s-6}\\} \\\\  & =5-4{e}^{6\\cdot t} \\\\  & =5-4{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we write down the solution to the initial value problem:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =[\\begin{aligned}{x}_{1}(t) \\\\ {x}_{2}(t)\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1+4{e}^{6t} \\\\ 5-4{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 5\\end{aligned}]+[\\begin{aligned}4 \\\\ -4\\end{aligned}]{e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}]}
